SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS

The code has been tested with python 3.12 on Ubuntu 22.04 and on 
Linux Mint 22.2

The software requires a backend (such as xTB), plus all the packages listed
in the dftscape-dxtb/pyproject.toml file.




INSTALLATION

in the dftscape-dxtb directory run:

curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh  # install uv
uv python install 3.12                           # setup the environment
uv sync
uv pip install -e .
uv pip install dxtb[libcint]

On a typical modern CPU it approximately takes 1 to 3 minutes to complete
the installation.




DEMO

in the dftscape-dxtb directory run:

source .venv/bin/activate                       # activate the environment
cd ./examples/ethane
python create_data.py ./data/ethane.xyz True    # create input data for 
												# k-HiSD using an xyz file
python landscape.py                             # run the automated saddle
												# search method

The output will be saved in the dftscape-dxtb/examples/ethane/outputs
directory.

On a typical modern CPU it will take 1-2 minutes to find the eclipsed
and staggered ethane conformations. 




INSTRUCTION FOR USE 

The data we show in the main text of the paper is stored in the 
dftscape-dxtb/results/borazine_graph.json, which contains all the found
transition states, local minima and dissociated molecules.

Our presented data can be reproduced by following all the steps in the
"DEMO USAGE" section, with "ethane" substitued for "borazine".